[
    {
        "content": "<p>The equality <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.card_inertia_eq_ramificationIdxIn#doc\">docs#Ideal.card_inertia_eq_ramificationIdxIn</a> between the cardinality of the inertia subgroup and the ramification index requires the extension of residue fields to be separable (in order for the cardinality of the automorphism group of residue fields to equal the degree).</p>\n<p>Currently <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Ideal.card_inertia_eq_ramificationIdxIn#doc\">docs#Ideal.card_inertia_eq_ramificationIdxIn</a> requires <code>[PerfectField p.ResidueField]</code>, but in the review of <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41591\">#41591</a> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"488648\">@Xavier Roblot</span> and I have been discussing whether it might make sense to add a more specific typeclass such as</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasSeparableResidueFieldsAt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">isSeparable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">LiesOver</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsSeparable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and perhaps also</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">HasSeparableResidueFields</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">CommRing</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Prop</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">isSeparable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Ideal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">B</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsPrime</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">Algebra</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">IsSeparable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">under</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">A</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">P</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ResidueField</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Does something like this seem reasonable?</p>",
        "id": 616941100,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1786967317
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is there a way to make it work for <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsLocalRing.ResidueField#doc\">docs#IsLocalRing.ResidueField</a> as well? Not prescribing an approach, but perhaps taking the residue field as a parameter and requiring isomorphism, or redefining the local ring residue field to be defeq to maximalIdeal.ResidueField?</p>",
        "id": 616941926,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1786967624
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Well, if you applied <code>Algebra.HasSeparableResidueFieldsAt</code> to the maximal ideals of an extensions of local rings, then you would get a predicate on <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=IsLocalRing.ResidueField#doc\">docs#IsLocalRing.ResidueField</a>.</p>",
        "id": 616942329,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1786967776
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As for taking the residue field in as a parameter, I'm not so sure that's a good idea. I think the predicate should just refer to the rings, but then you could deduce separability for arbitrary choices of residue field as a consequence.</p>",
        "id": 616942557,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1786967850
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Good point! This assumption is also used in the comparison of two definitions of ramification index (index of valuation group vs index of inertia subgroup).</p>",
        "id": 617124551,
        "sender_full_name": "Jz Pan",
        "timestamp": 1787038416
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think the fixed-<code>p</code> version <code>…At p</code> is probably what we want for <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41591\">#41591</a>, since the results there are about a fixed prime <code>p</code>. It would make things nicer in this PR for sure. </p>\n<p>On the design, I agree it is better to phrase it in terms of the rings. One small thing about the global version: since <code>⊥</code> is a prime ideal with <code>⊥.ResidueField = Frac B</code>, quantifying over all primes  would also require <code>Frac B / Frac A</code> to be separable, which is perhaps more than one wants in characteristic p. Maybe restricting to maximal ideals would avoid that.</p>",
        "id": 617403517,
        "sender_full_name": "Xavier Roblot",
        "timestamp": 1787132662
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't have a good sense of the correct global predicate in the non-Dedekind domain context, so maybe it would be better to just add the <code>At</code> version for now.</p>",
        "id": 617406125,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1787133363
    }
]